'''''Daucus carota''''', whose [[common name]]s include '''wild carrot''',<ref name="Blamey">{{cite book | last1=Blamey | first1=Marjorie | last2=Grey-Wilson | first2=C. | title=The Illustrated Flora of Britain and Northern Europe | publisher=Lubrecht & Cramer Limited | publication-place=London | date=1989-01-01 | isbn=0-340-40170-2 | page=286}}</ref><ref name="Streeter">{{cite book | last=Streeter | first=David | title=Flower Guide | publisher=Collins | publication-place=London | date=2010 | isbn=978-0-00-718389-0 | page=358}}</ref> '''European wild carrot''', '''bird's nest''', '''bishop's lace''', '''carrot flower''', and '''Queen Anne's lace''' (North America), is a [[flowering plant]] in the family [[Apiaceae]]. It is native to [[temperate regions]] of Afro-Eurasia with a number of regional subspecies, and is [[naturalisation (biology)|naturalised]] widely elsewhere.<ref name="841063-1" >{{cite POWO|id=841063-1|title=''Daucus carota'' L.|access-date=20 March 2022}}</ref> [[Carrot]]s cultivated as a food crop are [[cultivar]]s of the domesticated subspecies ''Daucus carota'' subsp. ''sativus''.

==Description==
The wild carrot is a [[Herbaceous plant|herbaceous]], somewhat variable [[biennial plant]] that grows to {{cvt|30|–|100|cm|ft|sigfig=1}}, rarely {{cvt|120|cm|ft|sigfig=1}} tall,<ref name="Streeter"/><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.omafra.gov.on.ca/english/crops/facts/ontweeds/wild_carrot.htm|title=Ontario Weeds: Wild carrot|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061220074051/http://www.omafra.gov.on.ca/english/crops/facts/ontweeds/wild_carrot.htm|archive-date=20 December 2006}}</ref><ref name=":2">{{Cite book|last=Spellenberg|first=Richard|url=https://archive.org/details/nationalaudubons00spel/page/338/|title=National Audubon Society Field Guide to North American Wildflowers: Western Region|publisher=Knopf|year=2001|isbn=978-0-375-40233-3|edition=rev|pages=338|orig-date=1979}}</ref> and is roughly hairy, with a stiff, solid [[Plant stem|stem]]. The leaves are [[Pinnation|tripinnate]], finely divided and lacy, and overall triangular in shape. The leaves are {{Convert|5-15|cm|abbr=on|frac=4}} long,<ref name=":2" /> bristly and alternate in a [[pinnate]] pattern that separates into thin segments. The flowers are small and dull white, clustered in flat, dense [[umbel]]s. The umbels are terminal and about {{cvt|3|-|6|in|cm|0|disp=flip}} wide.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|url=https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/all/daucus-carota/|title=Daucus carota|website=plants.ces.ncsu.edu|access-date=31 March 2017}}</ref><ref name=":2" /> They may be pink in bud and may have one (rarely a few) pink, reddish or purple flower (the "ruby") in the centre of the umbel.<ref name="Blamey"/> There are 7–13 [[bract]]s below the umbel; these are three-forked or pinnate, which distinguishes the plant from other white-flowered [[umbellifer]]s.<ref name="Streeter"/> As the seeds develop, the umbel curls up at the edges, becomes more congested, and develops a concave surface. The fruit is a small, dry, bumpy, oval and flattened [[carpel]] 2.5–4&nbsp;mm long, with short [[Style (botany)|styles]] and hooked spines, as well as protective hairs surrounding them;<ref name="Streeter"/><ref>{{cite book|title=The Pocket Guide to Wild Flowers|author1=McClintock, David|author2=Fitter, R. S. R.|publisher=Collins|year=1956|page=103}}</ref><ref name=":0"/> it is [[Bicarpellatae|bicarpellate]], with two mericarps. Its [[endosperm]] grows before the [[embryo]].<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Wurtele|first1=E S|last2=Wang|first2=H|last3=Durgerian|first3=S|last4=Nikolau|first4=B J|last5=Ulrich|first5=T H|title=Characterization of a gene that is expressed early in somatic embryogenesis of ''Daucus carota''.|journal=Plant Physiology|date=May 1993|volume=102|issue=1|pages=303–312|doi=10.1104/pp.102.1.303|pmid=8108498|pmc=158776 |bibcode=1993PlanP.102..303W }}</ref> The dried umbels can detach from the plant, and have been suggested to have [[tumbleweed]] characteristics.<ref name="Faulkner1917">{{cite book|title=The Mysteries of the Flowers|url=https://archive.org/details/mysteriesofflowe00fauluoft|author=Faulkner, Herbert Waldron|publisher=Frederick A. Stokes|year=1917|page=[https://archive.org/details/mysteriesofflowe00fauluoft/page/210 210]}}</ref>{{Better source needed|date=June 2025}} The function of the tiny red flower, due to [[anthocyanin]], is to attract insects. The flowers are mainly produced from June to August,<ref name="Blamey"/><ref name="Streeter"/> rarely also in May or September.<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":2" />

===Identification===
''D.{{nbsp}}carota'' is similar in appearance to the deadly [[Conium maculatum|poison hemlock]], but is readily distinguished by a mix of [[Pinnation|tripinnate]] leaves, fine hairs on its solid green stems and its leaves, a root that smells like carrots, and often a single red flower in the centre of the umbel.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://kingcounty.gov/en/legacy/services/environment/animals-and-plants/noxious-weeds/weed-identification/poison-hemlock.aspx|title=Noxious weeds: Poison-hemlock|website=King County, Washington}}</ref><ref>{{EMedicine|article|821362|Hemlock Poisoning}}</ref> Hemlock also differs in having purple mottling on its stems, which also lack the hairiness of the plain green wild carrot stems.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://ravensroots.org/blog/2015/6/26/poison-hemlock-id|title=How to Tell the Difference Between Poison Hemlock and Queen Anne's Lace|first=Gabe|last=Garms|website=Raven's Roots|date=2 July 2015}}</ref> Even more distinctive is the unpleasant smell of the crushed leaves of hemlock which is like mouse urine.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Dauncey |first1=Elizabeth A. |last2=Larsson |first2=Sonny |date=2018 |title=Plants That Kill: A Natural History of the World's Most Poisonous Plants |location=Princeton, New Jersey |publisher=Princeton University Press |pages=100−101 |isbn=978-0-691-17876-9 |oclc=1027707097}}</ref> 

