===Function of the dark central floret===
The function of the central dark floret of ''D.&nbsp;carota'' has been subject to debate since [[Charles Darwin]] speculated that it is vestigial.<ref>Darwin, Charles. ''The Different Forms of Flowers on Plants of the Same Species. --''. London: JMurray, 1877.</ref> Some have suggested that it has the adaptive function of mimicking insects, thus either discouraging herbivory<ref>Detto, C. "Blutenbiologische Untersuchungen, I. Uber Die Beteutung Der Insektenahnlichkeit Der Ophrysblute Nebst Bemerkungen Uber Die Mohrenblute Dei Daucus Carota". ''Flora, Jena'' 94 (1905): 287–329.</ref> or attracting pollinators<ref>Rothschild, M. "Some Observations on the Relationship between Plants, Toxic Insects and Birds". In ''Phytochemical Ecology'', edited by J.B. Harborne, 1–12. London: Academic Press, 1972.</ref> by indicating the presence of food or opportunities for mating.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Yeo|first1=P.F.|title=Miscellaneous notes on pollination and pollinators|journal=Journal of Natural History|date=December 1972|volume=6|issue=6|pages=667–686|doi=10.1080/00222937200770621|bibcode=1972JNatH...6..667Y}}</ref> One study in Portugal found that the dark florets contributed to visitation by the varied carpet beetle ''[[Anthrenus verbasci]]'', and that higher numbers of dark florets correlated with increased visitation, whereas inflorescences without dark florets had fewer visits. Replacing the dark florets with one or more freeze-killed ''A.&nbsp;verbasci'', which are similar to the florets in size and shape, produced similar results to observations of inflorescences with intact florets.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Goulson|first1=David|last2=Mcguire|first2=Kate|last3=Munro|first3=Emma E.|last4=Adamson|first4=Susan|last5=Colliar|first5=Louise|last6=Park|first6=Kirsty J.|last7=Tinsley|first7=Matthew C.|last8=Gilburn|first8=Andre S.|title=Functional significance of the dark central floret of Daucus carota (Apiaceae) L.; is it an insect mimic?|journal=Plant Species Biology|date=August 2009|volume=24|issue=2|pages=77–82|doi=10.1111/j.1442-1984.2009.00240.x|bibcode=2009PSBio..24...77G }}</ref>

