== Taxonomy ==
The carrot was first officially [[species description|described]] by [[Carl Linnaeus]] in his 1753 work ''[[Species Plantarum]]''.<ref name="Linnaeus 1753" /> In 2016, an international team sequenced the full genome of ''Daucus carota''.<ref>{{Cite web|date=9 May 2016|title=Carrot Genome Sequenced|url=https://www.genomeweb.com/sequencing-technology/carrot-genome-sequenced}}</ref>

===Subspecies===
The cultivated carrot derives from the wild carrot; both are the same species ''Daucus carota'' [[Carl Linnaeus|L]].<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Banga|first1=O.|date=February 1957|title=Origin of the European cultivated carrot|journal=Euphytica|volume=6|issue=1|pages=54–63|doi=10.1007/BF00179518|bibcode=1957Euphy...6...54B |s2cid=39884390}}</ref>

''D.&nbsp;carota'' subsp. ''carota'' is the type subspecies, native across temperate areas of the Old World and since naturalised in most temperate regions. Many subspecies of ''D.&nbsp;carota'' have evolved in different [[climate]]s. The type subspecies and all wild subspecies have consistently white roots, with a thin taproot with a bitter taste and woody centre. The middle umbellet of ''D.&nbsp;carota'' subsp. ''carota'' is not well developed (unlike in ''D.&nbsp;carota'' subsp. ''sativus'') and the centre flower varies from red to deep purple.<ref name=Baranski2012>{{cite journal|last1=Baranski|first1=Rafal|last2=Maksylewicz-Kaul|first2=Anna|last3=Nothnagel|first3=Thomas|last4=Cavagnaro|first4=Pablo F.|last5=Simon|first5=Philipp W.|last6=Grzebelus|first6=Dariusz|date=February 2012|title=Genetic diversity of carrot (Daucus carota L.) cultivars revealed by analysis of SSR loci|journal=Genetic Resources and Crop Evolution|volume=59|issue=2|pages=163–170|doi=10.1007/s10722-011-9777-3|doi-access=free|bibcode=2012GRCEv..59..163B }}</ref>

The cultivated carrot (the domesticated subspecies ''D.&nbsp;carota'' subsp. ''sativus'') has been found to be most closely related to Central Asian examples of the type subspecies, indicating a single domestication event and supporting the historical record of its origin there. 

''D.&nbsp;carota'' subsp. ''sativus'' has roots that can be a wide range of colours, with a thicker root and sweeter taste. The [[Whorl (botany)|whorl]] of barbs above the spine on the vallecular ridges of the [[Schizocarp|mericarp]] of ''D.&nbsp;carota'' subsp. ''sativus'' mature very well,{{clarify|date=July 2021}} and the middle umbellet is well developed.<ref name=Baranski2012 />

