== Distribution and habitat ==
''Daucus carota'' is native to [[temperate regions]] of Europe, northern Africa (south to Ethiopia), southwestern and eastern Asia;<ref name="841063-1" /> it has also been introduced to and become naturalised in North and South America, Australia, New Zealand, and South Africa. It is commonly found in meadows, along roadsides and in unused fields. It thrives best in sun to partial shade.<ref name=":0" />

== Toxicity ==
Like many other species of Apiaceae, the leaves of the wild carrot may cause [[phytophotodermatitis]]; skin contact with the foliage, especially wet foliage, can cause [[Irritation|skin irritation]] in some people, so caution should be used when handling the plant.<ref>{{EMedicine|article|1119566|Phytophotodermatitis|clinical}}</ref><ref name="USFWS 2017">{{cite web|url=https://medium.com/@USFWS/dont-touch-these-plants-six-lookalikes-you-want-to-avoid-edbe291b69e4|title=Don't touch these plants! Six lookalikes you want to avoid|date=19 July 2017|publisher=U.S. Fish & Wildlife Service|access-date=8 September 2018}}</ref><ref name="Purdue 2017">{{cite web|title=Daucus carota|url=https://www.hort.purdue.edu/newcrop/duke_energy/Daucus_carota.html#Toxicity|access-date=21 April 2017|website=hort.purdue.edu}}</ref> It may also have a mild effect on [[horse]]s.<ref>{{Cite web|last=LLC|first=HorseDVM|title=Queen anne's lace Poisoning in Horses|url=http://www.horsedvm.com/poisonous/queen-annes-lace|website=HorseDVM}}</ref>

The compound [[falcarinol]] is naturally found in ''Daucus carota'' for protection against fungal diseases. Laboratory tests show the compound is toxic to mice and to the water flea ''[[Daphnia magna]]''.<ref name=":1">{{Cite journal|last1=Crosby|first1=D.G.|last2=Aharonson|first2=N.|date=January 1967|title=The structure of carotatoxin, a natural toxicant from carrot|journal=Tetrahedron|volume=23|issue=1|pages=465–472|doi=10.1016/S0040-4020(01)83330-5|pmid=6037290}}</ref> Normal consumption of carrots has no toxic effect in humans.<ref>{{cite book|author=Deshpande|title=Handbook of Food Toxicology|publisher=CRC Press|year=2002|isbn=978-0-8247-0760-6|location=Hyderabad, India}}</ref>

== Uses ==
Like the cultivated carrot, the wild carrot root is edible while young, but quickly becomes too woody to consume.{{citation needed|date=December 2020}} The flowers are sometimes battered and fried. The leaves and seeds are also edible.<ref name=":0"/>

The seeds and flowers have been used as a method of [[contraception]] and an [[abortifacient]] for centuries, but scientific research has not confirmed any such effects and there is no evidence of safety.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Jansen|first1=Gabrielle Claire|last2=Wohlmuth|first2=Hans|title=Carrot seed for contraception: A review|journal=Australian Journal of Herbal Medicine|date=January 2014|volume=26|issue=1|pages=10–17|url=https://search.informit.org/doi/10.3316/INFORMIT.138844085450714}}</ref> If used as a [[dyestuff]], the flowers give a creamy, off-white colour.

===Association with other plants===
This species can be used as a [[companion plant]] to crops. Like most members of the umbellifer family, it attracts [[wasp]]s to its small flowers in its native land, but where it has been introduced, it attracts very few wasps. In northeast Wisconsin, it did succeed in attracting butterflies and wasps when introduced with blueberries.<ref>{{cite book|author1=Laurie Neverman|title=Queen Anne's Lace – Butterfly Host Plant and Blueberry Protector|url=https://commonsensehome.com/queen-annes-lace/|date=24 June 2017}}</ref> In western Washington grazed grasslands dominated by ''[[Carex inops]]'', it was the most frequently-visited flower by the ochre ringlet butterfly ''Coenonympha california.''<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Bussan |first1=Samantha K. |last2=Schultz |first2=Cheryl B. |date=2023-12-12 |title=Can cattle grazing contribute to butterfly habitat? Using butterfly behavior as an index of habitat quality in an agroecosystem |journal=Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution |volume=11 |article-number=1162060 |doi=10.3389/fevo.2023.1162060 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2023FrEEv..1162060B |issn=2296-701X}}</ref> This species is also documented to boost [[tomato]] plant production when kept nearby, and it can provide a [[microclimate]] of cooler, moister air for [[lettuce]], when [[intercropping|intercropped]] with it.<ref>{{cite book|last1=Philbrick|first1=Helen|last2=Gregg|first2=Richard B.|title=Companion Plants and How to Use Them|date=August 1, 1966|publisher=[[Devin-Adair Publishing Company]]|isbn=978-0815952107|oclc=2323470|page=58|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=qbzkDAAAQBAJ|quote=Lettuce likes strawberries, is aided by the presence of carrots and makes radishes tender in summer.}}</ref> But Iowa, Michigan, and Washington have listed it as a [[noxious weed]],<ref>{{cite web|url=https://plants.usda.gov/home/plantProfile?symbol=DACA6|website=[[USDA]]|title=Plant Profile – Daucus carota L. (Queen Anne's lace)|access-date=11 June 2007}}</ref> and it is considered a serious pest in pastures. It persists in the [[soil seed bank]] for two to five years.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Clark|first1=D. L.|last2=Wilson|first2=M. V.|title=Post-dispersal seed fates of four prairie species|journal=[[American Journal of Botany]]|date=1 May 2003|volume=90|issue=5|pages=730–735|doi=10.3732/ajb.90.5.730|pmid=21659169|doi-access= |bibcode=2003AmJB...90..730C }}</ref>

===Taste===
Several different factors can cause the root of a carrot to have abnormal [[metabolite]]s (notably [[6-Methoxymellein|6-methoxymellin]]) that can cause a bitter taste in the roots. For example, carrots have a more bitter taste when grown in the presence of [[apple]]s. Also, [[ethylene]] can easily produce stress, causing a bitter taste.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Coxon|first1=David T.|last2=Curtis|first2=R.Frank|last3=Price|first3=Keith R.|last4=Levett|first4=Gordon|title=Abnormal metabolites produced by Daucus carota roots stored under conditions of stress|journal=Phytochemistry|date=August 1973|volume=12|issue=8|pages=1881–1885|doi=10.1016/S0031-9422(00)91505-X|bibcode=1973PChem..12.1881C}}</ref>

==Culture==
Wild carrot was introduced into [[North America]] by European settlers and has become common; it is often known as "Queen Anne's lace" there. [[Anne, Queen of Great Britain]] is the queen after whom the plant is reputedly named, though as a plant name it is not known before 1895, 180 years after her death.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://lace.lacefairy.com/Lace/Fun/QueenAnnsLace.html|title=Queen Ann's Lace|access-date=10 November 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120621054245/http://lace.lacefairy.com/Lace/Fun/QueenAnnsLace.html|archive-date=21 June 2012|url-status=dead}}</ref> It is so called because the [[inflorescence]] resembles [[lace]], prominent in fine clothing of the day; the red flower in the centre is said to represent a droplet of blood where Queen Anne pricked herself with a needle when she was making the lace.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbg.org/article/weed_of_the_month_queen_annes_lace|title=Weed of the Month: Queen Anne's Lace|author=Saara Nafici|date=21 August 2014|publisher=Brooklyn Botanic Garden}}</ref>

===History through artwork===
The history of ''Daucus carota'' and its cultivation in different parts of the world can be traced through historical texts and artwork. Paintings from the 16th and 17th centuries, for example, of maids in a market or farmers' most recent crops can provide information on carrots' history. Such paintings show that yellow or red roots were cultivated in [[Turkey]], [[North Africa]], and [[Spain]]. Orange roots were cultivated in 17th-century [[Netherlands]].<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Zeven|first1=A. C.|last2=Brandenburg|first2=W. A.|title=Use of paintings from the 16th to 19th centuries to study the history of domesticated plants|journal=Economic Botany|date=October 1986|volume=40|issue=4|pages=397–408|doi=10.1007/BF02859650|bibcode=1986EcBot..40..397Z |s2cid=24391862}}</ref>

===References in poetry===
"[[wikisource:Sour Grapes/Queen-Ann's-Lace|Queen Anne's Lace]]" is the title and subject of a poem by [[William Carlos Williams]] in his 1921 collection ''[[Sour Grapes (poetry collection)|Sour Grapes]].''

